-2.1 The closed positive path ball is complete: a Cauchy sequence converges uniformly by [F3], its uniform limit is continuous, and the positive cone is closed because $\nu^-(E)\le\|\nu-\nu_j\|_{TV}$ when $\nu_j\ge0$. It is nonempty because it contains the constant path $\nu^0_t=\mu_0$. If $q<1$ is the contraction coefficient in step 1.2, iteration $\nu^{j+1}=\Phi(\nu^j)$ from $\nu^0$ satisfies $\|\nu^{j+1}-\nu^j\|\le q^j\|\nu^1-\nu^0\|$. For $m>n$, $\|\nu^m-\nu^n\|\le\|\nu^1-\nu^0\|\sum_{j=n}^{m-1}q^j\le q^n\|\nu^1-\nu^0\|/(1-q)\to0$, so completeness gives a limit. The contraction bound makes $\Phi$ continuous, hence the limit is fixed; if two fixed points existed, their distance would be at most $q$ times itself, hence zero. Any other positive solution satisfies the same mild formula: for its path $\zeta$, multiply $\dot\zeta=G(\zeta)-a_\zeta\zeta$ by the bounded integrating factor $e^{\int_0^t a_\zeta(s,\cdot)ds}$ and integrate in weighted total variation. Continuity at $0$ puts it in this ball on a sufficiently short interval, where the fixed-point uniqueness just proved makes it equal to the constructed solution. [F3, step 1.2, algebra]
